#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
wspy-testpoint -- run selection / role-assignment, aggregation, and README rendering for a test point +
machine (INVESTIGATION.md's "Test-point-level curated performance-summary README deep-dive",
Tier 3 item 5, pieces 1-4).

A test point's linked runs are rarely interchangeable repeats: some are genuine repeated trials, some
are redos superseding an earlier bad run, and some dive into more detail with a different collection
scope (extra process-tree/IBS/symbol-sample passes) meant to supplement the primary numbers rather than
be pooled into the same statistical bucket. `select-runs` resolves, for one (suite, benchmark, machine)
combination, which role each run plays -- stats-pool member, supplementary source, or excluded/
superseded -- plus which single run (if any) is "primary" for narrative purposes, and persists that
decision into doc/REPORT_HIERARCHY.md's report-root tree as runs.json.

A default role/primary is computed for any run not already recorded with human_set=true; a human's own
--set/--primary choice from a prior invocation is never silently overwritten by a later default-
heuristic pass -- the same source-pointer-vs-commentary separation the 4.1 curation studio already
established for per-run blocks (new_block(), web/server.py), applied here one level up: to which runs
feed a report, not which artifacts within one run do.

`aggregate` turns a resolved stats-pool run set into real statistics via `wspy-summary --run-id
<hostname>:<run_id>` (one per present run) -- not `--command`/`--hostname`, which can't distinguish a
redo (case 1 above) from the runs it's redoing when they share identical command text, the single most
common reason a test point ever needs role-assignment in the first place. Requires the runs already be
ingested into the target `wspy-store` database (never auto-ingests -- a run only has a run-index record
if it was launched with `--run-index` in the first place, so there's no reliable way to synthesize one
retroactively); missing runs are warned about, not silently dropped.

`render` turns the same aggregated statistics into an actual curated `README.md`, reusing
`web/server.py`'s existing curation-studio block model rather than inventing a second one: each
counter-group section (topdown, cache, branch, ...) is written to a small generated markdown file
under `sections/`, referenced by an ordinary `"artifact"` block in a `curation.json` right alongside
`runs.json` -- the exact same `source_file`-points-at-regenerable-content-plus-separate-`commentary`
split `select-runs` already uses for run roles, just one level further down. A block for a section that
already existed from a prior render keeps its title/depth/commentary/position exactly as a human left
them; only the section file's own content refreshes. `load_curation()`/`save_curation()`/
`export_block_content()`/`render_export_markdown()` (all `web/server.py`, imported the same way as
`aggregate`'s `load_run_history_entry()`) work completely unmodified here -- they only ever take a
plain directory path, never assume `--output-root`'s own layout.

Stdlib-only (matches wspy-analyze/wspy-bundle/wspy-queue/wspy-publish's existing "thin client, no
dependency" convention).
"""
import argparse
import csv
import io
import json
import os
import socket
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import sys
from collections import Counter

